\f1\fs22 Chanta met most of the band in the large windowless living area downstairs. It had four TVs on the walls (three of which had no bullet holes in their screens), two large lounge suites against the walls, a long beerhall-style table, and a full kitchen at the far end. A small bar occupied a corner for anyone who couldn\'92t be bothered to walk one room over, which was also a bar.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Most of the band and crew sat at the table eating fried squirrel and Pepsi-Cola salad. A Flaming Star Series Elvis named Pacer and a Girl Happy Series named Rusty lounged in separate couches, each with a doting Elvisette fan snuggling close-in, their legs over their man\'92s lap.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s skin crawled when he saw the enormous python curled up on the floor by the bar. It was one of the crazier gifts from a devoted Elvisette, one of the burgeoning fan clubs of Elvines devoted to the Great Teacher. \'93I thought we\'92d agreed to get rid of the snake,\'94 Chanta said somewhat wearily.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>An Easy Come Easy Go Series named Ted got up from the table, took an easy stroll over to the bar and plucked a can of cola from the bar fridge. Tapped the snake with his shoe. It didn\'92t move. \'93It\'92s got itself a belly full of rats and mice,\'94 Ted said. \'93Seems to me it\'92s doing a community service.\'94 The Ghetto was overrun by vermin and even brand new constructions like the American Sound Studios suffered for it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta had to concede Ted\'92s point. \'93I\'92d rather put the snake to service than the coffin I received the other day.\'94 He received all kinds of gifts from Elvisettes\'97teddy bears; flowers; jewellery; love letters; puppies; and kittens that the snake was suspected of eating. Of all the crazy gifts, he would never have dreamt anyone would send him a coffin. \'93We threw that out, right?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Burned it,\'94 one of the crew said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s eyes were drawn to Roslyn, the stunning blonde draped over Pacer\'92s lap, until Valerie\'97the Elvisette attending Rusty\'97winked and smiled at him. Chanta had dallied with Valerie a week or so before, but now he felt\'85 what was it? Valerie seemed lovely to him now, in the same way Betty Lee was looking lovelier\'97perhaps even more beautiful than Roslyn, who Chanta had thought was the most stunning of all. He found it odd, but supposed the more similar two people looked, the more you noticed their differences. Maybe Valerie\'92s blonde hair reminded him of Sarah\'97the thought came with a pang. But why was he more attracted to Valerie than Roslyn? She just seemed\'85 brighter\'85 somehow.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Any new lyrics, Great Teacher?\'94 Rusty asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta broke from his reverie, and made a thing of searching for a coffee cup in a kitchen cabinet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Great Teacher?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Huh?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Any new lyrics?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ah\'85 er\'85 yeah.\'94 Chanta poured the last from the coffee pot. \'93Indeed. Quite a few\'85 big ideas\'85 running around in my head. Nothing finalised yet\'97I mean, not down on paper.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The mood in the room deflated. The breakfasters dropped their squirrel bones on the plates and mopped the corners of their mouths. \'93Great Teacher,\'94 a Speedway Series named Steve said. \'93We\'92ve laid down the instrumental arrangements for over thirty tunes.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I, um\'85 sure. Just give me the day, okay?\'94 Chanta mumbled. \'93I think I just need to get out of here for a while.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I wouldn\'92t,\'94 Pacer warned. \'93There were thousands of Elvisettes out there when we came in this morning.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yeah,\'94 said Jodie, a Kissin\'92 Cousins Series. \'93They\'92re bringing pillows and blankets and sleeping on the sidewalk. Some are bringing tents.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They seem kinda fun,\'94 Chanta countered, and smiled for the four most recent Elvisettes to grace his bed. Betty Lee, Marguerita, Princess Shalimar, and the other one smiled back. Marguerita glugged a litre of orange juice from the bar fridge. \'93A bunch of them climbed up on my balcony and sang me a song this morning.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'94If you go out there, you won\'92t reach the next block before they rip your clothes off,\'94 Steve warned.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta sat sullenly on a bench at the table.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Squirrel?\'94 Steve offered Chanta a chunk of meat that looked exactly nothing like a chicken leg.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No thanks. I\'92ve lost my appetite.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92ve been mulling over an idea for a song,\'94 Rusty offered. A chorus of groans and shout-downs rose up in the room.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Don\'92t make me shoot another TV,\'94 Steve said. \'93Nothing\'92s special about your shoes. We all wear blue suede.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93But mine are so comfortable and supple. I love them. They were hand-crafted in Tel Aviv.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So what\'92s the song? Viva Lost Bagels?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No, listen.\'94 Rusty brushed Valerie away and grabbed a guitar from a stand near the couch. He strummed. Satisfied with the tone and the tuning, he sang:\
